\title{Phantom Topologies and the Phantom Number:\\ Reality as the Consensus of Observers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We introduce and study \emph{phantom topologies}: a framework in which a single set
carries a family of topologies indexed by ``observers,'' and the \emph{real} topology
is defined to be the topology on which all observers unanimously agree. Formally, a
phantom topology on a set $X$ with observer set $\Obs$ is a map
$T:\Obs\to\{\text{topologies on }X\}$, and its \emph{consensus} topology is the
supremum $\bigsqcup_{o} T(o)$ in the lattice of topologies --- the topology whose open
sets are exactly the sets open in every $T(o)$. Each observer is finer than the
consensus, so the consensus operation is order-reversing in resolution: enlarging the
observer set can only coarsen the shared reality. We call a representation
\emph{genuinely phantom} when each observer is strictly finer than the consensus, and
define the \emph{phantom number} of a space as the least number of such strictly-finer
observers whose consensus reproduces it. We prove that the Euclidean line has phantom
number two --- it is the consensus of the lower-limit and upper-limit topologies --- and
we \emph{refute} the conjecture that non-metrizable spaces require at least three
observers: the two-point indiscrete space is non-metrizable yet is the consensus of two
strictly-finer Sierpi\'nski observers. The refutation shows that separation strength and
phantom number are independent invariants.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The physics of measurement suggests a provocative geometric question: what if the
topology of a space depended on the observer? This paper answers it rigorously. We
treat ``reality'' not as a single privileged topology but as the \emph{agreement} of
many observer-relative topologies. The mathematical content lies in the interplay
between two very different measurements of a space:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Separation} --- how well the topology distinguishes points, governed
  by the separation axioms and, at the strong end, by metrizability; and
  \item \textbf{Phantom number} --- how many strictly-finer topologies must be
  intersected to recover the space, a purely order-theoretic (lattice) quantity.
\end{itemize}
A natural conjecture links them: since metrizable spaces are highly separated and the
Euclidean line reconstructs from two observers, perhaps \emph{less} separated
(non-metrizable) spaces require \emph{more} observers. We prove this conjecture false
with a minimal counterexample and argue that the two invariants are orthogonal.

\subsection*{Conventions on the lattice of topologies}
Topologies on a fixed set $X$ are ordered by \emph{fineness}: we write $s\le t$ to mean
$s$ is \emph{finer} than $t$, i.e.\ every $t$-open set is $s$-open. Under this convention
the finest (discrete) topology is the bottom element and the coarsest (indiscrete)
topology is the top element $\top$. The topologies on $X$ form a complete lattice; for a
family $(t_i)_{i\in I}$ the supremum $\bigsqcup_i t_i$ has as its open sets exactly those
open in \emph{every} $t_i$:
\[
  U \text{ is } \Big(\textstyle\bigsqcup_i t_i\Big)\text{-open}
  \iff \forall i,\ U \text{ is } t_i\text{-open.}
\]
The supremum in the fineness order is the \emph{coarsest} topology all $t_i$ refine, and
its opens are the \emph{intersection} of the open-set families. This models ``consensus.''

\section{The phantom-topology framework}

\begin{definition}[Phantom topology]
A \emph{phantom topology} on a set $X$ with observer set $\Obs$ is a function
$T:\Obs\to\{\text{topologies on }X\}$ assigning to each observer $o$ a topology $T(o)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consensus / real topology]
The \emph{consensus} topology of $T$ is $\cons(T)=\bigsqcup_{o\in\Obs} T(o)$, the
supremum in the lattice of topologies.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Agreement]
For every $U\subseteq X$, $U$ is $\cons(T)$-open iff $U$ is $T(o)$-open for every
observer $o$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the description of the supremum of topologies: its opens are exactly the
sets open in every $T(o)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Measurement coarsens]
For every observer $o$, $T(o)\le\cons(T)$; each observer is finer than the consensus.
Consequently, enlarging the observer set never sharpens reality.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$T(o)$ is a term of the supremum, so $T(o)\le\bigsqcup_{o'}T(o')$ by the universal
property. Adding index terms can only move the supremum upward (coarser).
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Genuinely phantom representation]
A phantom topology $T$ is a \emph{genuinely phantom representation} of a topology $\tau$
if $\cons(T)=\tau$ and $T(o)<\tau$ (strictly finer) for every observer $o$. Strictness
means each observer resolves \emph{phantom} open sets --- open to that observer but not in
reality.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Phantom number]
The \emph{phantom number} of $(X,\tau)$ is the least cardinality of an observer set
admitting a genuinely phantom representation of $\tau$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[One observer is never phantom]\label{rem:one}
For a single-observer family, $\cons(T)=T(*)$, since the supremum of a one-element family
is that element. Hence a one-observer representation of $\tau$ forces $T(*)=\tau$, not
strictly finer. Thus a genuinely phantom representation needs at least two observers, and
the finite phantom number is at least $2$.
\end{remark}

\section{The real line as a two-observer consensus}

\begin{definition}[Lower- and upper-limit observers]
The \emph{lower-limit (Sorgenfrey) observer} $L$ declares $U\subseteq\R$ open iff every
$x\in U$ has some $b>x$ with $[x,b)\subseteq U$. The \emph{upper-limit observer} $R$
declares $U$ open iff every $x\in U$ has some $a<x$ with $(a,x]\subseteq U$.
\end{definition}
These define topologies: $\R$ is open (step by $\pm1$); finite intersections use the
minimum (resp.\ maximum) of endpoints; arbitrary unions are immediate.

\begin{lemma}[Phantom witnesses]\label{lem:phantomR}
$[0,1)$ is $L$-open but not Euclidean-open, and $(0,1]$ is $R$-open but not
Euclidean-open.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $x\in[0,1)$, $[x,1)$ witnesses $L$-openness; but any ball around $0$ contains points
$<0$, so $[0,1)$ is not Euclidean-open. Symmetrically for $(0,1]$ under $R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-observer theorem for $\R$]\label{thm:R}
A set $U\subseteq\R$ is Euclidean-open iff it is open for both $L$ and $R$; equivalently
$L\sqcup R=\tau_{\mathrm{Euclid}}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Suppose $U$ is $L$- and $R$-open, $x\in U$. Take $b>x$ with
$[x,b)\subseteq U$ and $a<x$ with $(a,x]\subseteq U$; set
$\varepsilon=\min(x-a,b-x)>0$. If $|y-x|<\varepsilon$: for $y\ge x$, $y\in[x,b)\subseteq U$;
for $y<x$, $y\in(a,x]\subseteq U$. So $B(x,\varepsilon)\subseteq U$ and $U$ is
Euclidean-open. ($\Rightarrow$) If $U$ is Euclidean-open and $x\in U$, pick
$\varepsilon>0$ with $B(x,\varepsilon)\subseteq U$; then $[x,x+\varepsilon)\subseteq U$
and $(x-\varepsilon,x]\subseteq U$ give $L$- and $R$-openness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Phantom number of $\R$ is two]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:phantomR} both observers are strictly finer than
$\tau_{\mathrm{Euclid}}$, so $(L,R)$ is a genuinely phantom two-observer representation:
phantom number $\le 2$. By Remark~\ref{rem:one} it is $\ge 2$. Hence the phantom number of
$\R$ is exactly $2$. Moreover $L\neq R$, since $[0,1)$ is $L$-open but not $R$-open.
\end{corollary}

\section{Refuting the metrizability barrier}

\begin{definition}[$T_0$ / Kolmogorov]
A space is $T_0$ if any two distinct points are separated by an open set containing one
but not the other.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}\label{fact:metrT0}
Every metrizable space is $T_0$: distinct $p\ne q$ have $d(p,q)>0$, so $B(p,d(p,q))$
excludes $q$. Contrapositively, a non-$T_0$ space is not metrizable.
\end{fact}

\begin{definition}[Indiscrete two-point space]
Let $X=\{\mathsf{true},\mathsf{false}\}$ with the indiscrete topology $\top$, whose only
open sets are $\varnothing$ and $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Non-metrizability]
The indiscrete two-point space is not metrizable.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
It is not $T_0$: the only nonempty open set is $X$, so no open set separates
$\mathsf{true}$ from $\mathsf{false}$. By Fact~\ref{fact:metrT0} it is not metrizable.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Sierpi\'nski observers]
On $X=\{\mathsf{true},\mathsf{false}\}$ define
$S_{\mathsf{true}}$: $U$ open iff $(\mathsf{false}\in U\Rightarrow\mathsf{true}\in U)$,
with opens $\{\varnothing,\{\mathsf{true}\},X\}$; and
$S_{\mathsf{false}}$: $U$ open iff $(\mathsf{true}\in U\Rightarrow\mathsf{false}\in U)$,
with opens $\{\varnothing,\{\mathsf{false}\},X\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Phantom singletons]\label{lem:sing}
$\{\mathsf{true}\}$ is $S_{\mathsf{true}}$-open (vacuous hypothesis) and
$\{\mathsf{false}\}$ is $S_{\mathsf{false}}$-open; neither is indiscrete-open.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Two-observer consensus for the indiscrete space]\label{thm:ind}
$S_{\mathsf{true}}\sqcup S_{\mathsf{false}}=\top$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$U$ is consensus-open iff open for both observers, i.e.
$(\mathsf{false}\in U\Rightarrow\mathsf{true}\in U)\wedge(\mathsf{true}\in U\Rightarrow
\mathsf{false}\in U)$, equivalently $\mathsf{true}\in U\Leftrightarrow\mathsf{false}\in U$:
$U$ contains both points or neither, so $U\in\{\varnothing,X\}$, which is $\top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict refinement]
$S_{\mathsf{true}}<\top$ and $S_{\mathsf{false}}<\top$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:sing}, so
$(S_{\mathsf{true}},S_{\mathsf{false}})$ is a genuinely phantom two-observer
representation of the indiscrete space.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Refutation]
The indiscrete two-point space is non-metrizable yet has phantom number $2$. Hence the
conjecture ``every non-metrizable space requires at least three observers'' is false.
\end{theorem}

\section{Discussion: separation and phantom number are orthogonal}

Theorems~\ref{thm:R} and~\ref{thm:ind} assign phantom number $2$ to spaces at opposite
extremes of geometric structure: the exquisitely separated Euclidean line and the
maximally unseparated indiscrete pair. The failed conjecture conflates two independent
axes. \emph{Separation} measures how well points can be told apart (the $T_0$--$T_1$--$T_2$
hierarchy, culminating in metrizability); it is geometric and distance-flavored.
\emph{Phantom number} measures meet-reducibility in the lattice of topologies --- how many
strictly-finer topologies must be intersected to recover $\tau$; it is order-theoretic.
The indiscrete example shows a maximally non-separated space can be the meet of two
strictly-finer topologies: each Sierpi\'nski observer contributes one phantom singleton,
and the two phantoms are complementary, composing to a biconditional that forces
triviality in consensus. Non-metrizability lives on the separation axis; low phantom
number lives on the reducibility axis; the example decouples them.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{1. The phantom number is a lattice invariant, not a geometric one.} The minimum
number of sharper observers reconstructing a space should equal the depth to which its
topology splits into strictly finer topologies that intersect back to it --- finite exactly
when the topology is not irreducible under such splitting. Exact values (2 for the line, 2
for the indiscrete pair) at opposite extremes of geometry point to a lattice-theoretic
common thread.

\textbf{2. Separation and phantom number are independent.} For every target phantom number
$k\ge2$ and every separation strength, there should exist a space realizing exactly that
combination; the two invariants can be dialed independently.

\textbf{3. Adding observers can only blur, never sharpen.} The consensus operation should
be order-reversing and meet-continuous on arbitrary observer diagrams, turning ``reality as
consensus'' into a functorial principle.

\textbf{4. Every second-countable space is a two-observer consensus.} Any space with a
countable base that is not irreducible under splitting should be reconstructible by two
sharper observers, built by adjoining one-sided half-neighborhoods to each basic open on
the left and on the right.

\section{Conclusion}

We formalized observer-relative geometry through phantom topologies, defined the consensus
topology as the supremum in the lattice of topologies, and introduced the phantom number as
the least number of strictly-finer observers whose consensus recovers a space. The
Euclidean line has phantom number two; the non-metrizable indiscrete two-point space also
has phantom number two, refuting the conjectured metrizability barrier. Phantom number and
separation are independent invariants --- the former order-theoretic, the latter geometric ---
and ``reality as consensus'' is reducibility in the lattice of topologies.

\end{document}

